#{[17592186090604 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186090607] [17592186090610 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186090612] [17592186092512 :question/worked-solution "The chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm df}{\\mathrm dx}=e^{3x}\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dx}(3x)=3e^{3x}.\n$$"] [17592186092733 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186402978] [17592186092734 :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the outer exponential and multiply by the derivative of its exponent:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}=e^{x^4+1}\\cdot4x^3=4x^3e^{x^4+1}.\n$$"] [17592186092735 :question/worked-solution "For a constant base, $\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dx}a^{u(x)}=a^{u(x)}\\ln(a)u^{\\prime}(x)$. With $u(x)=6x^2-4$,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}=3^{6x^2-4}\\ln(3)\\cdot12x.\n$$"] [17592186093806 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186093807] [17592186094093 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186094096] [17592186094681 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186094684] [17592186095424 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186095425] [17592186095472 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186095474] [17592186095664 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186095669] [17592186096972 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3x^5-4x^2$. The chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=3u^2u^{\\prime}\\\\&=3(3x^5-4x^2)^2(15x^4-8x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186097020 :question/worked-solution "On an interval avoiding $x=1$, rearrange and integrate:\n\n$$\ny^{\\prime}=-\\frac2{(x-1)^2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=\\frac2{x-1}+C.\n$$\n\nThe condition $y(2)=1$ gives $1=2+C$, so $C=-1$. Thus\n\n$$\ny=\\frac2{x-1}-1,\\qquad x>1.\n$$\n\nThe interval $(1,\\infty)$ is the maximal interval containing the initial point."] [17592186097118 :question/worked-solution "Rearrange as $f^{\\prime}(x)=-11x$ and integrate:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\int-11x\\,\\mathrm dx=-\\frac{11}{2}x^2+C.\n$$"] [17592186097250 :question/worked-solution "With $u=x^4+3x$, we have $f=u^2$ and $u^{\\prime}=4x^3+3$. Therefore\n\n$$\nf^{\\prime}(x)=2(x^4+3x)(4x^3+3).\n$$"] [17592186097258 :question/worked-solution "Write $y=(4x-3)^{1/2}$ and apply the chain rule:\n\n$$\ny^{\\prime}=\\frac12(4x-3)^{-1/2}\\cdot4=\\frac2{\\sqrt{4x-3}}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative is defined for $x>3/4$."] [17592186097290 :question/worked-solution "Rearrange and integrate:\n\n$$\nf^{\\prime}(x)=-\\frac12e^x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f(x)=-\\frac12e^x+C.\n$$\n\nUsing $f(0)=-1$ gives $-1=-1/2+C$, hence $C=-1/2$. Therefore\n\n$$\nf(x)=-\\frac12(e^x+1).\n$$"] [17592186097300 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186398890] [17592186097301 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186398896] [17592186097302 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186392709] [17592186097807 :tutorial/content "Let's now prove the chain rule in the following form:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = f'(g(a)) \\cdot g'(a)\n$$\n\nLet $f$ and $g$ be functions such that\n\n- $g$ is differentiable at $a$,\n\n- $f$ is differentiable at $g(a)$.\n\nThen $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $a$, and we compute its derivative as follows.\n\nWe start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a}\n$$\n\nSince $g$ is differentiable at $a$, it is also continuous there. Hence $g(x) \\to g(a)$ as $x \\to a$. For $x$ sufficiently close to $a$, we have $g(x) \\ne g(a)$, so we may write\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a} \n= \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{g(x) - g(a)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(a)}{x - a}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $x \\to a$, and using the product rule for limits (which applies since both limits exist), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘g)^{′}(a) & =\\lim_{x\\to a}(\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot \\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a} \\\\  & ={f}^{′}(g(a))\\cdot {g}^{′}(a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required."] [17592186122996 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186123001] [17592186125066 :question/worked-solution "Inhibited growth is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the current population. The capacity is $85$, so the model is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm dR}{\\mathrm dt}=k(85-R),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nThe initial population $20$ determines the initial condition $R(0)=20$; it does not change the limiting capacity."] [17592186149839 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186149842]}
